WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.440
You are listening to episode six of the Calm Competitor,

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:07.920
where we tackle the infamous phenomenon of paralysis by analysis.

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:09.039
In this episode,

00:00:09.039 --> 00:00:18.799
we'll learn the art of chunking and how giving your conscious mind a tiny immediate job allows your automatic muscle memory to perform flawlessly.

00:00:18.799 --> 00:00:20.000
How winners?

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:21.840
Stop thinking about winning.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:23.120
Picture this.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:25.680
You are standing at the end of a diving board,

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:27.360
three meters above the water.

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.280
The pool is turquoise.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.840
Still, you can see the drain at the bottom.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:34.319
Your feet grip the rough fiberglass edge.

00:00:34.319 --> 00:00:36.560
Your coach is watching from the bleachers.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:38.479
The meet starts in forty minutes.

00:00:38.479 --> 00:00:41.439
You have dived this entry hundreds of times,

00:00:41.439 --> 00:00:43.119
in practice, alone,

00:00:43.119 --> 00:00:44.799
with music in your ears.

00:00:44.799 --> 00:00:47.040
You know it. Your body knows it.

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:49.119
And yet you're standing here thinking,

00:00:49.119 --> 00:00:50.880
what if I mess up the entry?

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:52.479
What if I bend my knees?

00:00:52.479 --> 00:00:55.680
What if the splash is huge and embarrassing?

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:58.880
What if I lose points and it costs the team,

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:02.720
and everyone watches me climb out of the pool knowing it was me?

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:05.120
What if your feet shift?

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:07.279
Something in your legs goes tight,

00:01:07.279 --> 00:01:08.720
and for one absurd,

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:13.680
awful moment you have almost forgotten how to do a thing you have done five hundred times.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:15.519
What just happened to your brain?

00:01:15.519 --> 00:01:20.639
And why does thinking harder about doing something well sometimes make you worse at it?

00:01:20.639 --> 00:01:26.319
That question turns out to be one of the most productive puzzles in the science of how humans perform.

00:01:26.319 --> 00:01:28.559
The answer is not what you would guess,

00:01:28.559 --> 00:01:30.080
and once you understand it,

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:34.080
you will see something completely different when you watch anyone compete.

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:36.160
Most people, if you ask them,

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:39.440
would say that performing well is about thinking clearly.

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:40.959
The better your focus,

00:01:40.959 --> 00:01:42.319
the better you do.

00:01:42.319 --> 00:01:43.519
That sounds right.

00:01:43.519 --> 00:01:46.800
It has the reasonable feeling of a thing that should be true.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.120
The brain is in charge of the body.

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:52.080
More brain involvement should mean more precision.

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:53.839
A surgeon concentrates.

00:01:53.839 --> 00:01:55.919
A chess player concentrates.

00:01:55.919 --> 00:02:01.760
So an athlete concentrating hard on every detail of their movement should logically do better.

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:04.319
When something goes wrong in a performance,

00:02:04.319 --> 00:02:06.319
a stumble, a missed note,

00:02:06.319 --> 00:02:07.839
a fumbled catch,

00:02:07.839 --> 00:02:10.160
the instinct is almost always the same.

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:12.479
Pay more attention next time.

00:02:12.479 --> 00:02:13.759
Focus harder.

00:02:13.759 --> 00:02:15.600
Think it through more carefully,

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:17.199
and it almost works.

00:02:17.199 --> 00:02:18.320
For beginners,

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:19.919
this is exactly right.

00:02:19.919 --> 00:02:22.479
When you are first learning to shoot a free throw,

00:02:22.479 --> 00:02:24.639
conscious thought is your ally.

00:02:24.639 --> 00:02:30.880
You need to think about elbow angle and wrist flick and follow through because none of it is automatic yet.

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:32.880
The brain guides the hands.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:34.559
But as skill develops,

00:02:34.559 --> 00:02:37.919
something quietly changes in where the competence lives,

00:02:37.919 --> 00:02:40.559
and that change is where things get complicated.

00:02:40.559 --> 00:02:42.240
Here is where it breaks.

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:43.679
In two thousand one,

00:02:43.679 --> 00:02:46.320
the psychologist CN Bailock,

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:56.960
a researcher who spent her career studying the precise moment that The task was simple.

00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:59.679
Put a ball into a hole a short distance away.

00:02:59.679 --> 00:03:01.440
These were experienced players.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:03.360
The putt should have been easy.

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:05.440
One group was told to swing normally,

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:09.199
the other group was told to monitor their putting stroke as they swung,

00:03:09.199 --> 00:03:13.119
specifically, to notice the moment the club made contact with the ball.

00:03:13.119 --> 00:03:15.839
The second group putted significantly worse,

00:03:15.839 --> 00:03:16.960
not beginners,

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:18.479
not people still learning,

00:03:18.479 --> 00:03:20.960
experts. The monitoring instruction,

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:24.720
the explicit order to pay close attention to a skill they already had,

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:25.919
degraded it.

00:03:25.919 --> 00:03:27.360
Their accuracy dropped,

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:29.440
their timing changed.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:33.199
Something about the act of watching themselves closely broke something.

00:03:33.199 --> 00:03:36.240
Bailock called this paralysis by analysis.

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:38.479
The name implies a dramatic failure,

00:03:38.479 --> 00:03:42.960
but the actual mechanism is quieter and stronger than any collapse.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:44.880
Nothing was lost from memory.

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:46.559
The skill was intact.

00:03:46.559 --> 00:03:48.479
The damage was not to the knowledge,

00:03:48.479 --> 00:03:50.880
it was to how the knowledge was accessed.

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:53.440
And that distinction is the entire puzzle.

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:57.199
Why would accessing your own skill differently make it worse?

00:03:57.199 --> 00:04:00.559
Here is what the brain actually does with a learned movement.

00:04:00.559 --> 00:04:02.960
When you first learn to do something physical,

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:04.399
dribble a basketball,

00:04:04.399 --> 00:04:05.600
type on a keyboard,

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:10.080
ride a bike, you use a part of the brain system that's good at conscious,

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:11.759
deliberate control.

00:04:11.759 --> 00:04:12.800
This system,

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:16.000
centered in regions of the prefrontal cortex,

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:18.480
the front part of the brain that handles reasoning,

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:20.079
planning, and attention,

00:04:20.079 --> 00:04:21.200
is slow,

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:23.119
effortful, and serial,

00:04:23.119 --> 00:04:25.600
meaning it handles one thing at a time.

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:28.559
It learns by making errors and correcting them,

00:04:28.559 --> 00:04:30.880
and it talks to itself constantly.

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:32.399
You can almost hear it.

00:04:32.399 --> 00:04:33.440
Left hand,

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:35.200
right hand, balance,

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:37.040
pedal, don't fall.

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:39.279
That is learning and it is expensive.

00:04:39.279 --> 00:04:41.279
It takes enormous mental energy.

00:04:41.279 --> 00:04:44.320
But something happens after hundreds of hours of practice.

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:46.399
The movement gets handed off.

00:04:46.399 --> 00:04:47.760
Not erased,

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:50.720
moved. It migrates to a different system,

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:55.839
one built around a brain structure called the basal ganglia and the cerebellum.

00:04:55.839 --> 00:04:57.600
These structures don't think in words.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:04:59.040
They don't reason,

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:00.559
they are pattern engines.

00:05:00.559 --> 00:05:03.519
They execute sequences of action fluidly,

00:05:03.519 --> 00:05:07.839
automatically, without needing the prefrontal cortex to supervise.

00:05:07.839 --> 00:05:10.559
This is what muscle memory actually is.

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:12.640
The muscles have no memory.

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:17.119
What has changed is where in the brain the motor program is stored and run.

00:05:17.119 --> 00:05:20.399
The program has been transferred to a system that's fast,

00:05:20.399 --> 00:05:22.000
parallel and quiet.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:24.720
It handles multiple things simultaneously,

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:26.000
without effort.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:30.320
When a professional pianist plays a piece they've performed a thousand times,

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:33.679
the prefrontal cortex is not controlling their fingers.

00:05:33.679 --> 00:05:41.119
The cerebellar basal ganglia circuit is running the sequence The prefrontal cortex is free to feel the music,

00:05:41.119 --> 00:05:42.559
respond to the room,

00:05:42.559 --> 00:05:45.119
breathe. And here is the trap.

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:50.160
About every movement you're about to make,

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:56.160
you are pulling the execution back out of the automatic system and trying to run it again through the slow,

00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:58.559
deliberate, conscious system.

00:05:58.559 --> 00:06:01.440
The one that hasn't touched the skill in years,

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:03.920
the one that is not built for it anymore.

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:10.239
You are trying to fly the plane manually when the autopilot has been running it perfectly for a thousand hours.

00:06:10.239 --> 00:06:14.320
The override introduces errors the autopilot would never make.

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:16.959
This is where the second layer becomes visible.

00:06:16.959 --> 00:06:18.559
The automatic system,

00:06:18.559 --> 00:06:24.399
the cerebellum and basal ganglia running your rehearsed skills needs one thing to function,

00:06:24.399 --> 00:06:25.839
to be left alone.

00:06:25.839 --> 00:06:27.679
It doesn't respond to instruction,

00:06:27.679 --> 00:06:29.920
it responds to cues.

00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:32.239
It runs when conditions are familiar,

00:06:32.239 --> 00:06:33.920
when attention is elsewhere,

00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:36.480
when the conscious mind is not watching.

00:06:40.079 --> 00:06:41.279
Because pressure,

00:06:41.279 --> 00:06:42.959
the awareness of stakes,

00:06:42.959 --> 00:06:44.160
of consequences,

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:45.359
of being watched,

00:06:45.359 --> 00:06:47.359
does something very specific.

00:06:47.359 --> 00:06:49.359
It directs attention inward.

00:06:49.359 --> 00:06:51.119
It makes you watch yourself.

00:06:51.119 --> 00:06:53.119
And the moment you start watching yourself,

00:06:53.119 --> 00:06:57.519
execute a skill you have automated you have triggered the override.

00:06:57.519 --> 00:07:04.000
A soccer player who has practiced penalties for five years can put a ball in the top corner almost without thinking,

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:09.600
but ask them to describe in words what their kicking foot does at the moment of contact,

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:11.760
and their next kick may go wide.

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:16.160
The act of verbalizing the skill forces it back through the conscious system,

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:20.480
like translating a fluent sentence into a language you learned last year.

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:22.720
The pressure doesn't erase the skill,

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:24.959
it redirects attention to the skill,

00:07:24.959 --> 00:07:28.000
and the redirection is what causes the failure.

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:30.959
Baylock documented this across many domains,

00:07:30.959 --> 00:07:32.000
putting in golf,

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:33.760
shooting free throws,

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:36.480
solving math problems under time stress,

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:38.959
taking tests when the stakes feel high.

00:07:38.959 --> 00:07:40.399
The same architecture,

00:07:40.399 --> 00:07:41.679
the conscious system,

00:07:41.679 --> 00:07:42.799
trying to help,

00:07:42.799 --> 00:07:47.519
inserting itself into an automatic process and making Now,

00:07:47.519 --> 00:07:49.279
connect the two layers.

00:07:49.279 --> 00:07:55.040
The brain has two modes of executing learned skill deliberate and automatic.

00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:56.959
Deliberate is for learning.

00:07:56.959 --> 00:07:58.959
Automatic is for performing.

00:07:58.959 --> 00:08:02.160
The failure mode of expertise is not forgetting.

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:05.279
It is accidentally switching modes at the wrong time.

00:08:05.279 --> 00:08:07.600
Pressure triggers self-monitoring.

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:09.839
Self-monitoring triggers the switch.

00:08:09.839 --> 00:08:13.600
And the switch for any well preceded skill makes it worse.

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:15.760
And here is what changes everything.

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:19.119
If the problem is attention redirected to execution,

00:08:19.119 --> 00:08:22.320
then the solution is to give attention somewhere else.

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:25.359
Something to attend to that is not the movement itself.

00:08:25.359 --> 00:08:28.239
This is what chunking actually does in the brain,

00:08:28.239 --> 00:08:31.119
not as a productivity trick or a pep talk,

00:08:31.119 --> 00:08:33.599
but as a neurological operation.

00:08:33.599 --> 00:08:37.599
When you break a performance into small immediate process cues,

00:08:37.599 --> 00:08:38.960
not win this match,

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:40.159
but watch the ball,

00:08:40.159 --> 00:08:41.520
not don't mess up,

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:42.559
but one breath,

00:08:42.559 --> 00:08:43.679
then release.

00:08:43.679 --> 00:08:46.000
You are not just calming yourself down.

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:48.960
You are providing the conscious system with a task,

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:50.479
something to hold,

00:08:50.479 --> 00:08:54.880
a job. And when the conscious system is occupied with the cue,

00:08:54.880 --> 00:08:56.320
the small, immediate,

00:08:56.320 --> 00:08:57.679
achievable thing,

00:08:57.679 --> 00:09:00.880
it is not watching the automatic system run the skill.

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:02.640
The autopilot gets to fly.

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:04.479
Think about it this way.

00:09:04.479 --> 00:09:06.320
When you learn to ride a bike,

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:09.119
someone probably told you to look where you wanted to go,

00:09:09.119 --> 00:09:10.479
not at the wheel.

00:09:10.479 --> 00:09:12.960
That instruction wasn't just about balance.

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:17.200
It was about giving your prefrontal cortex something useful to do,

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:18.400
a target.

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:20.159
Because if you looked at the wheel,

00:09:20.159 --> 00:09:22.719
you'd start consciously managing the wheel,

00:09:22.719 --> 00:09:27.679
and the automatic balancing system your body was building would get constantly interrupted.

00:09:27.679 --> 00:09:30.239
Process cues work the same way.

00:09:30.239 --> 00:09:32.080
Watch the seams on the ball.

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:33.760
Plant your left foot first.

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:35.200
Keep your chin down.

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:37.520
These are not reminders about mechanics.

00:09:37.520 --> 00:09:39.039
If you've practiced enough,

00:09:39.039 --> 00:09:41.520
the mechanics are in the automatic system.

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:45.359
These are occupying the conscious system so it stops interfering.

00:09:45.359 --> 00:09:47.359
The runner who counts strides,

00:09:47.359 --> 00:09:51.599
the gymnast who focuses on a spot on the wall during a beam routine,

00:09:51.599 --> 00:09:55.119
the batter who narrows attention to the pitcher's release point.

00:09:55.119 --> 00:09:56.799
They are not thinking less.

00:09:56.799 --> 00:09:58.880
They are thinking about something small,

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:00.640
immediate and external,

00:10:00.640 --> 00:10:04.559
which is neurologically different from thinking about their own performance.

00:10:04.559 --> 00:10:06.640
Two things are now true at once.

00:10:06.640 --> 00:10:12.559
First, the athletes who perform best under pressure are not the ones trying hardest to perform well.

00:10:12.559 --> 00:10:17.679
They are the ones who have learned to occupy the part of the brain that would otherwise interfere.

00:10:17.679 --> 00:10:22.400
Second, this is why just focus is almost useless advice.

00:10:22.400 --> 00:10:24.000
Focus on what?

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:26.799
If you tell a nervous performer to focus harder,

00:10:26.799 --> 00:10:29.200
they usually focus harder on the outcome,

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:30.719
on whether it's working,

00:10:30.719 --> 00:10:32.239
on what failure would mean,

00:10:32.239 --> 00:10:36.640
and they have now handed the conscious system exactly the wrong job.

00:10:36.640 --> 00:10:40.000
The practical resolution is almost absurdly simple,

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:43.760
and yet it is almost never what pressure tells you to do.

00:10:43.760 --> 00:10:45.760
Instead of asking how am I doing?

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:48.320
You ask a smaller question.

00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:50.559
What is the next immediate thing?

00:10:50.559 --> 00:10:51.919
Not the score,

00:10:51.919 --> 00:10:53.200
not the impression,

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:54.799
not the consequence,

00:10:54.799 --> 00:10:56.080
the next thing.

00:10:56.080 --> 00:10:57.280
One step,

00:10:57.280 --> 00:10:59.599
one breath, one movement,

00:10:59.599 --> 00:11:01.119
something achievable.

00:11:01.119 --> 00:11:03.840
When the next thing is genuinely small,

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:05.359
genuinely doable,

00:11:05.359 --> 00:11:08.719
the conscious system accepts the job and gets busy.

00:11:08.719 --> 00:11:10.320
And the automatic system,

00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:12.719
the fast one, the trained one,

00:11:12.719 --> 00:11:15.200
the one that holds five years of practice,

00:11:15.200 --> 00:11:17.200
runs without supervision.

00:11:17.200 --> 00:11:18.960
You are back on the diving board.

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:20.799
The pool is still turquoise,

00:11:20.799 --> 00:11:23.039
the drain is still visible at the bottom,

00:11:23.039 --> 00:11:25.200
your coach is still in the bleachers.

00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:28.320
But something is different about how you are standing here.

00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:30.559
You are not thinking about the splash,

00:11:30.559 --> 00:11:33.679
not running calculations about the judges' faces.

00:11:33.679 --> 00:11:35.200
You have one cue,

00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:36.239
one thing.

00:11:36.239 --> 00:11:38.080
Arms straight at the top.

00:11:38.080 --> 00:11:41.280
Just that. Your conscious mind wraps around that small,

00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:43.520
reachable thing and holds it.

00:11:43.520 --> 00:11:44.640
Elsewhere,

00:11:44.640 --> 00:11:47.599
quietly, in structures deeper than thought,

00:11:47.599 --> 00:11:49.919
the rest of the program is already loaded.

00:11:49.919 --> 00:11:52.080
The sequence you have rehearsed,

00:11:52.080 --> 00:11:56.159
the trained movements waiting in the system that does not need your help.

00:11:56.159 --> 00:11:57.840
You step off the board,

00:11:57.840 --> 00:12:00.400
and somewhere under the surface of that act,

00:12:00.400 --> 00:12:03.200
underneath the thing you are consciously doing.

00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:06.479
The part of your brain that learned all of this in the long,

00:12:06.479 --> 00:12:11.599
repetitive months of practice is running exactly as it was built to run.

00:12:11.599 --> 00:12:14.400
The conscious mind gives it one job,

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:16.640
hands off, lets it go.

00:12:16.640 --> 00:12:21.760
Which raises a question you might find yourself sitting with long after you climb out of the water.

00:12:21.760 --> 00:12:27.520
How many things are you already good at that you've been making harder by watching too closely?

00:12:27.520 --> 00:12:32.080
Now that you know how to break a massive challenge into manageable steps,

00:12:32.080 --> 00:12:36.640
how do you string them together into a reliable shield against anxiety?

00:12:36.640 --> 00:12:38.000
In episode seven,

00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:42.239
we'll look at the incredible neurological power of a pre-performance routine.

